#!/usr/bin/env python3
#  Project:      dfe-infra
#  File:         scripts/dfe-fleet
#  Purpose:      Ship scalo and rebuild its downstream fleet, via PR merge to main.
#  Language:     Python
#
#  License:      BUSL-1.1
#  Copyright:    (c) 2026 HYPERI PTY LIMITED
"""Fleet release helpers for scalo (Rust) and scalo-py (Python).

The compatibility entry point: the four subcommands below, unchanged, over
``hyperi_ai.suite`` -- ``tools/dfe_suite.py`` is the graph-driven one.

Four jobs, one tool::

    dfe-fleet ship-rs    [SUBJECT]              # scalo -> crates.io
    dfe-fleet ship-py    [SUBJECT]              # scalo -> PyPI
    dfe-fleet rebuild-rs APP_DIR VERSION [SUB]  # a Rust consumer
    dfe-fleet rebuild-py APP_DIR VERSION [SUB]  # a Python consumer

**Main is protected: nothing pushes to it.** Every repo the fleet touches now
carries a ``pull_request`` ruleset on its default branch, so a change lands as
a branch, a PR, and a squash merge. Approvals required: zero, so this still
runs completely unattended -- but ``git push origin main`` is gone for good.

The publish trigger is unchanged. hyperi-ci reads ``git log -1 --format=%B``
on ``refs/heads/main`` and looks for a ``Publish: true`` trailer, so the
SQUASH COMMIT MESSAGE we author carries the trailer onto main and the merge
event is itself the publish run. ``hyperi-ci publish`` (the workflow_dispatch
escape hatch) is used only where there is no new commit to land, because it
has silently created no run at all before now (2026-07-20). ``rebuild-rs
--release-unoptimized`` is the other dispatch: its per-run consent is a
workflow input, and a push event carries none.

Runs on macOS and Linux, on the guaranteed homedir venv: no jq, no BSD-vs-GNU
``stat``/``sed`` divergence, and no assumption about where the projects root
lives (see ``find_repo``). It needs ``git`` and ``gh`` on PATH, plus the
toolchain of whichever fleet half it is driving.

Every wait is an ACTIVE poll that early-fails the instant a job goes red, and
every wait has a hard deadline. A green run is never taken as proof of a
release: the live registry version has to actually move.
"""

